Subject: quartz: retain media probe handles until source loading completes
License: LGPL-2.1-or-later

Archive-backed Win32 file hooks can release their decoded stream cache when
its last handle closes. Quartz probes the media type before loading a source
filter, leaving a close/reopen gap that can invalidate the stream. A later
IAsyncReader::SyncRead can then return S_FALSE and graph construction can fail
with VFW_E_CANNOT_RENDER even though the original media is valid.

Allow get_media_type callers to take ownership of its probe handle, including
on an unsuccessful type match. RenderFile retains that handle across source
selection and the ASF fallback. AddSourceFilter retains it until the source
has loaded. Both release it on every success and failure exit. FileSource_Load
continues using a short-lived probe because it already owns the stream handle.

This applies to archive-backed VFS hooks regardless of application name or
media path. It complements quartz-filesource-vfs-fallback.patch, which handles
legacy file-size APIs and synchronous I/O. This patch targets the repository's
prepared CrossOver source and its existing ASF selection changes.

